//? Title: Kabsch alignment through the producing quaternion eigensystem
//? Subtitle: Proper rotations, actual paired residuals and the original noisy six-point cloud
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Rigid alignment maps corresponding source points to targets using a proper
rotation and translation. The native implementation uses Horn's quaternion
formulation of Kabsch's least-squares objective. We begin with an exact
synthetic transform, retain the original six-point forty-degree noisy example,
and inspect actual centroids, covariance, key matrix and principal eigenvector.
Degenerate and reflected controls expose uniqueness and proper-rotation limits.
Returned three-dimensional residuals, not an xy projection alone, measure fit.
)

\toc

> See the producing stages on four points

Rotate a tetrahedral cloud ninety degrees about z, then translate by (1,2,3).
Correspondence is known and fixed by input order. The following native analysis
captures the actual covariance and principal eigensystem used to form the
returned transform. The diagram summarizes these producing stages; it is not a
trace of Jacobi iteration steps or a second fit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kabsch_stages.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/kabsch_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::vector<dm::algorithms::Point3> s = {{0, 0, 0}, {1, 0, 0},
                                                   {0, 1, 0}, {0, 0, 1}};
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::Point3> t;
    for (auto p : s)
        t.push_back({-p[1] + 1, p[0] + 2, p[2] + 3});
    const auto a = dm::algorithms::kabsch_analyze(s, t);
    std::print("| coordinate | source centroid | target centroid | fitted translation |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", i,
                   a.source_centroid[i], a.target_centroid[i],
                   a.result.translation[i]);
    std::print("\n| component | actual quaternion | actual eigenvalue |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", i, a.quaternion[i],
                   a.eigenvalues[i]);
    std::print("\nActual RMSD {:.12e}; principal eigen residual {:.12e}.\n",
               a.result.rmsd, a.eigen_residual);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("pairs", {0, 0}, "Four paired points").node("center", {5, 0},
                                                       "Normalize and center").node("key", {10, 0}, std::format("Actual Horn key\nscale {:.4g}", a.covariance_scale)).node("fit", {15, 0}, std::format("Proper rigid fit\nRMSD {:.3g}", a.result.rmsd)).edge("pairs", "center").edge("center", "key").edge("key", "fit").save_svg("build/kabsch_stages.svg");
}
```
