> Derive translation, then rotation

For fixed rotation R, differentiating the squared residual loss gives the
optimal translation t=target_mean−R source_mean. Let centered source and target
points be x and y. With H=sum(x y transpose), the remaining objective is

$$
\sum_i\lVert R x_i-y_i\rVert^2=\sum_i(\lVert x_i\rVert^2+\lVert y_i\rVert^2)-2\operatorname{tr}(RH).
$$
\alttext(The centered squared alignment loss equals a rotation-independent norm sum minus twice the trace of rotation times cross-covariance.)

Horn's scalar-first unit quaternion q expresses trace(RH) as q transpose N q for
a symmetric four-by-four key matrix N. Maximizing this Rayleigh quotient selects
its largest-eigenvalue eigenvector. Normalizing q yields a proper rotation with
determinant +1, rather than an unrestricted reflection. The native Jacobi
eigensolver supplies the actual eigenpair; its residual is checked, and the
principal gap is reported as a diagnostic. A small or zero gap can indicate
nonuniqueness but is not an exact rank certificate.

The kernel independently scales each dataset by its maximum absolute coordinate
or one, centers and accumulates mean covariance in long double, then divides
that covariance by its largest absolute entry for the key construction. These
positive scalar normalizations preserve the exact objective's optimizing
rotation; they do not fit an extra scale. They reduce raw overflow, but floating
arithmetic and the eigensolver remain approximate and platform-dependent.

> Retain the original six-point experiment

The original source coordinates, forty-degree Rodrigues rotation about
normalized (1,1,1), translation (5,−2,1) and alternating amplitude .02 are
preserved by native fixture helpers. Its perturbations are deterministic
(+n,−n,+n) with alternating n, not independent Gaussian noise. The table reports
the actual returned rotation, translation and each three-dimensional residual.
The figure uses equal xy coordinate scales; it can hide errors in z.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kabsch_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/kabsch_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto s =
        dm::algorithms::kabsch_demo_source(), t =
        dm::algorithms::kabsch_demo_target();
    const auto a = dm::algorithms::kabsch_analyze(s, t);
    std::print("| row | R0 | R1 | R2 | translation |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", i,
                   a.result.rotation[i][0], a.result.rotation[i][1],
                   a.result.rotation[i][2], a.result.translation[i]);
    std::print("\n| original pair | target x | target y | target z | actual residual norm |\n| ---: | ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12e} |\n", i,
                   t[i][0], t[i][1], t[i][2], a.residual_norms[i]);
    std::print("\nRMSD per three-dimensional point {:.12e}; key eigen gap {:.12e}.\n",
               a.result.rmsd, a.eigen_gap);
    dm::algorithms::kabsch_xy_plot(a).title("Original paired cloud: actual xy alignment").save_svg("build/kabsch_original.svg");
}
```
