| control | pairs | RMSD | eigen gap |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| reflected tetrahedron | 4 | 2.000000000000e+00 | 0.000000000000e+00 |
| singleton | 1 | 0.000000000000e+00 | 0.000000000000e+00 |
| translated line | 3 | 0.000000000000e+00 | 0.000000000000e+00 |

Empty/mismatched inputs and a one-short complete pair ceiling reject.
\image(figures/kabsch_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A proper-rotation fit retains a residual on a reflected tetrahedron.)
\alttext(Source, reflected target and aligned projection illustrate an orientation reversal; projected overlaps alone do not establish a zero three-dimensional error.)

> Profile the deterministic perturbation amplitude

Using the original known rotation and translation without refitting gives
residual norm sqrt(3) times amplitude at every pair. A least-squares rigid refit
can improve on that feasible transform, but its RMSD is not an estimate of an
independent noise standard deviation here. Each row below runs the actual fit;
the reference curve is the known-transform residual for this specifically
constructed perturbation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kabsch_profile.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/kabsch_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, fit, known;
    std::print("| alternating amplitude | actual fitted RMSD | known-transform residual | principal eigen " "residual |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double amplitude : {0., .005, .01, .02, .05, .1}) {
        const auto a =
            dm::algorithms::kabsch_analyze(dm::algorithms::kabsch_demo_source(),
                                           dm::algorithms::kabsch_demo_target(
                                           amplitude));
        const double reference = std::sqrt(3.) * amplitude;
        if (a.result.rmsd > reference + 1e-11)
            throw std::runtime_error("feasible transform objective");
        std::print("| {:.3f} | {:.12e} | {:.12e} | {:.12e} |\n", amplitude,
                   a.result.rmsd, reference, a.eigen_residual);
        x.push_back(amplitude);
        fit.push_back(a.result.rmsd);
        known.push_back(reference);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, fit, "fitted", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, fit, "", {37, 99, 235},
                                                      4).line(x, known, "known",
                                                              {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, known, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4).title("Actual fit versus the known feasible transform").x_label("deterministic alternating amplitude").y_label("three-dimensional RMSD").y_limits(-.005, .22).save_svg("build/kabsch_profile.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| alternating amplitude | actual fitted RMSD | known-transform residual | principal eigen residual |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.000 | 1.896066778761e-16 | 0.000000000000e+00 | 2.108550677064e-19 |
| 0.005 | 7.984608436709e-03 | 8.660254037844e-03 | 1.954866896716e-19 |
| 0.010 | 1.597053572843e-02 | 1.732050807569e-02 | 2.178176145371e-19 |
| 0.020 | 3.194637621270e-02 | 3.464101615138e-02 | 2.701384480864e-19 |
| 0.050 | 7.990629171867e-02 | 8.660254037844e-02 | 1.609630061647e-19 |
| 0.100 | 1.599505759147e-01 | 1.732050807569e-01 | 2.608861477543e-19 |
\image(figures/kabsch_profile.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Fitted residuals are compared with a known feasible rigid transform.)
\alttext(The plot measures six actual refits and the constructed known-transform residual; neither curve is a stochastic confidence interval.)

All inputs must be finite, equal-sized and nonempty. Produced nonfinite values
or outputs outside finite double range throw; finite conversion may underflow to
zero. The captured aligned coordinates are rounded to double after widened
transform application, while captured residual norms precede that coordinate
rounding. Recomputing errors from the stored aligned coordinates can therefore
differ in last bits. The returned double transform itself is used when computing
RMSD.

The scalar fit uses linear work and constant auxiliary storage apart from fixed
four-by-four eigensolver allocations. Analysis additionally owns the original
pairs, aligned points and residual norms, with linear copied/output storage. Its
default complete cap is one million paired records, checked after input
validation and before copies; zero disables the optional cap. This count does
not describe the several output vectors, byte/capacity overhead or peak RAM.
Native plotting has a separate complete-pair ceiling and finite padded-span
domain. No external dataset is forced into this controlled alignment experiment.

Independent NumPy SVD, symmetric eigensystems and residual calculations checked
100 noisy rigid fits across scales 1e-150, 1 and 1e150, including the actual
covariance, Horn matrix, eigenvalues and proper rotation. Another 100 ordinary
fits matched the frozen original within the stated numerical tolerance; native
controls include reflections, degeneracy and ownership. The shared ten-report
build passed 131 native tests, 38 directly instrumented
address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public examples,
documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten representative
clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs executed successfully and
their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> References

Wolfgang Kabsch (1976), “A solution for the best rotation to relate two sets of
vectors”, Acta Crystallographica A32, 922–923,
[doi:10.1107/S0567739476001873|https://doi.org/10.1107/S0567739476001873].
Berthold K. P. Horn (1987), “Closed-form solution of absolute orientation using
unit quaternions”, JOSA A4, 629–642,
[doi:10.1364/JOSAA.4.000629|https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSAA.4.000629]. The
implementation follows the quaternion formulation for a proper rigid transform,
without Horn's optional scale estimation.
